module gunbc.scm.ancestry

// ANCESTRY: WHICH COMMIT A COMMIT CAME FROM.
//
// The model did not have this. Every `parent` in gunbc.scm is TREE-NODE parentage inside the object
// store -- an edge from a node to its child -- and none of it relates one commit to another. A
// RepositoryCommit is { root, message }, and `commits` is a flat List, so before this module a
// repository's history was a SEQUENCE and not a graph. Every operation git states in terms of
// ancestry -- merge-base, ahead/behind, commits-since, log ranges, and merge itself -- had no
// referent here at all.
//
// IT IS CARRIED, NOT RECOVERED FROM POSITION, and that is forced rather than chosen. Position gives
// a total order and says nothing about descent: that the commit at index 1 follows the one at index
// 0 in the list does not make it its child, and reading it that way would assume history is both
// linear and append-ordered -- neither of which the model establishes. Recovering ancestry from position
// would therefore not be recovering it; it would be inventing it, and the invention would be
// invisible because it would agree with position by construction.
//
// THIS MODULE LANDS BEFORE ANY PRODUCER, WHICH IS THE CHEAPEST MOMENT IT COULD. RepositoryCommit is
// constructed in exactly two places on main -- its own type home and a witness -- so no production
// code has yet baked in the assumption that history is a sequence. That is model-before-implement in
// the order DESIGN prescribes, not a type built speculatively ahead of its consumer.

import std.types { Bool, List }
import std.minted_identity { MintedId, minted_id_eq }

// THE ROOT IS A STRUCTURAL STATE, NOT AN EMPTY PARENT.
//
// The alternative -- a parent field holding some "nothing" value -- makes "this commit begins the
// history" and "this commit's parent is missing" the same value, and those have opposite meanings: the
// first is the ordinary state of every repository's first commit, the second is damage. That is the
// same conflation `log` refused when it made the empty history its own state rather than an empty
// row list, and it is refused here for the same reason and one layer deeper.
//
// EXACTLY ONE PARENT, AND THIS IS THE DECISION THAT WOULD BE UNWRITABLE LATER, so it is derived from
// what the model's own merge does rather than from what git does.
//
// gunbc.scm.merge `merge` takes ONE target Commit and a LIST OF PROPOSALS, and answers `Merged`
// carrying ONE commit. A Proposal is a set of authored requirements -- it is not a commit, it has no
// root, no message and no identity in history. So a merge in THIS model combines one commit with N
// things that were never commits, and the result has exactly one commit antecedent. Git's second
// parent exists because git merges two BRANCHES, which are two commits; that shape has no referent
// here, because nothing in this model can merge two commits at all.
//
// So a parent LIST would not be more general, it would be a state with no producer -- an arm nothing
// can construct, which DESIGN section 4b rules worse than absent because it gets cited as capability
// the model does not have. WIDENING TRIGGER, stated so the reason is checkable rather than trusted:
// if `merge` ever takes two Commits rather than one commit and N proposals, this coproduct gains a
// several-parents arm, and the change is a compile error at every consumer rather than a silent
// reinterpretation -- which is what makes single-parent a safe decision now rather than an
// irreversible one.
// WHICH OCCURRENCE IN THIS HISTORY -- NOT WHICH PROGRAM. This type exists because those are two
// different questions and the model previously answered both with `ObjectId`.
//
// THE BOUNDARY RULE, stated so the next author can place a new lookup without matching it against a
// list of sites: an `ObjectId` is content-addressed, so it genuinely identifies a PROGRAM, and
// keying an object by it is correct -- uniqueness is the POINT of that identity, not an assumption
// about it. A commit record is a program PLUS a message PLUS a position in history, and only the
// last of those is unique. So `ObjectId` is the right key for objects and the wrong key for commit
// records, and the question to ask at a new lookup is which of the two you are keying.
//
// THE HISTORY THAT DECIDES IT is an ordinary revert, not a corner case:
//
//   record 0:  root A,  RootCommit
//   record 1:  root B,  parent -> record 0
//   record 2:  root A,  parent -> record 1     (a revert to exactly the program in record 0)
//
// Content addressing CORRECTLY gives records 0 and 2 the same root. Root-keyed ancestry then needs
// A to be simultaneously `RootCommit` and `DescendsFrom{B}`, which is a contradiction at the level
// of the type -- one key, two irreconcilable values. The repository already admits this: `log`
// derives references from list POSITION precisely because two commits may share a root and differ
// only in message.
//
// WHAT THE OLD GRAIN DID, and it is why this is a correctness recut rather than a tidy-up: a
// first-match lookup answers with record 0's `RootCommit`, so a walk from record 2 terminates
// immediately and reports it as a root. The revert edge and record 1 vanish. Not a refusal -- a
// well-formed, confident, wrong answer, which is the silent wrongness DESIGN section 5 places
// outside the ladder entirely.
//
// A COMMIT REFERENCE IS A MINTED IDENTITY IN THE COMMIT SUBJECT SPACE, never a list position and
// never a digest of mutable commit facts. The subject parameter prevents a numeric coincidence with
// another allocator's ordinal from being compared as the same identity. The value is opaque outside
// the codec's membership index: log and status carry it but derive no order, recency, or content from
// its ordinal. This discharges the positional spelling's append-only obligation by removing its
// subject -- inserting, deleting, or reordering rows cannot retarget an existing reference.

// TWO SUCCESSOR LANES WAITED ON THIS CUT FOR DIFFERENT GROUNDS, even though the dependency event was
// the same. A commit producer waited on the HAZARD: authoring against positional references before
// an append-only wall existed would make silent retargeting reachable. Repository reading and its
// CLI surface carried no such mutation hazard; they waited on the VOCABULARY, because implementing a
// consumer against a representation already scheduled for replacement would cement the old root.
// If this cut ever slips or splits, a loader may correctly be called hazard-free and still must wait
// on the second ground. Conflating those grounds makes that true objection produce the wrong order.
type RepositoryCommitSubject

type RepositoryCommitRef {
  identity: MintedId<RepositoryCommitSubject>
}

fn repository_commit_ref_eq(left: RepositoryCommitRef, right: RepositoryCommitRef) -> Bool {
  minted_id_eq(left: left.identity, right: right.identity)
}

type CommitAncestry
  = RootCommit
  | DescendsFrom { parent: RepositoryCommitRef }

// AUTHOR AND TIMESTAMP ARE NOT DECIDED HERE, AND ARE EXPLICITLY SEPARATED RATHER THAN LEFT TO RIDE
// ALONG. They were reported absent beside `parent` by the log verb, and they are absent for a
// DIFFERENT REASON, which is why they cannot be settled in this change:
//
//   PARENT is a STRUCTURAL fact. It relates two things the model already holds, it is decidable
//   inside the model, and nothing external is consulted to know it. That is why it can land now.
//
//   AUTHOR and TIMESTAMP are EXTERNAL OBSERVATIONS -- who ran the command, and what the clock said.
//   Neither is derivable from the graph, and DESIGN is explicit that external reality is observed,
//   typed, and admitted or refused, never fabricated. This repository has no modelled clock and no
//   modelled process identity to observe them WITH, and acquiring either is the same frozen-host
//   question that gates the store binding and process output.
//
// So modelling them here would mean inventing two facts to fill fields, which is the fabricated-
// plausible-output failure at the exact boundary a commit is supposed to be trustworthy. They are
// blocked on a ruling; parent was not. Recording that difference is the point of this note.


// The history this walk may traverse, supplied as the commit identities the repository holds. It is
// a PROJECTION of the envelope's own commit list rather than a second authority: nothing here stores
// history, and a caller that passed a different population would be describing a different
// repository, not corrupting this one.
type AncestryStep {
  at: RepositoryCommitRef
  ancestry: CommitAncestry
}

// ONE CARRIER, SO THERE IS NOTHING FOR TWO AUTHORITIES TO DISAGREE ABOUT.
//
// The walk previously took a `history` list AND a `lookup` function, and that pairing was the defect:
// `history` was consumed only as the fold's sequence, its element bound to `_unused`, so it supplied
// the STEP BUDGET and nothing else while every membership question was answered by `lookup`. Two
// authorities for one fact (DESIGN section 3), and they were free to disagree:
//
//   an EMPTY history folded zero times, so the accumulator's INITIAL value survived as the verdict
//   and the walk announced a revisit having observed nothing at all;
//
//   a start ABSENT from history but known to lookup traced normally, so the arm named
//   `AncestryStartNotInHistory` was decided by a lookup fact rather than the history fact it names;
//
//   a parent ABSENT from history but known to lookup was traversed instead of reaching `BrokenAt`.
//
// A membership check before the fold would have closed the first of those and left the third
// constructible -- it concedes the bad state is writable and guards one instance. The carrier removes
// those three outright instead: membership, relation and step bound all derive from these rows,
// because there is no second place to look. That is the whole of what this shape buys, and an earlier
// revision of this annotation claimed more -- that it made the disagreement UNREPRESENTABLE.
//
// IT DOES NOT, AND THE RESIDUAL STATE IS NAMED HERE BECAUSE THIS IS THE CARRIER THE FACT IS ABOUT.
// Two steps may carry the same `at`, and `history_step_at` resolves by first match, so one carrier
// can disagree WITH ITSELF under a duplicated key -- the same first-match class this change removed,
// one level down. Uniqueness is an invariant of whatever BUILDS the carrier, not of the type: it has
// no producer in production code today, and it stays authorable in a fixture, which is the honest
// rung (DESIGN section 4b) -- mechanically prevented at each builder rather than structurally
// impossible. A uniqueness CHECK would be the wrong repair, for exactly the reason this paragraph
// already gives about membership checks: it would concede the duplicate is writable and guard one
// instance of it. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: an unforgeable-construction wall making one admitted builder the
// sole constructor of RepositoryHistory, at which point a fixture cannot express the collision.
type RepositoryHistory {
  steps: List<AncestryStep>
}

// The single question the walk asks: is there a row at this reference, and what does it say. It
// replaces the `lookup` parameter, and it cannot disagree with the step budget because the budget is
// this same list.
fn history_step_at(history: RepositoryHistory, at: RepositoryCommitRef) -> AncestryStep? {
  fold(history.steps, init: none, f: fn(found, step) {
    match found {
      Present { value: _ } => found
      Absent =>
        if repository_commit_ref_eq(left: step.at, right: at) {
          Present { value: step }
        } else {
          none
        }
    }
  })
}

// THREE OUTCOMES, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE A NAIVE WALK GETS WRONG BY LOOPING FOREVER.
//
// AncestryTraced carries the chain from the starting commit back to the root, inclusive and in that
// order, so a consumer reads descent without re-deriving it.
//
// AncestryBrokenAt names a commit whose parent is not in this history. That is damage, and it is
// distinguished from reaching the root because they are opposite facts: one is a complete history,
// the other is a truncated one, and a walk that answered "done" for both would report a broken chain
// as a finished one.
//
// AncestryStartNotInHistory is the caller's fact, not the history's, and separating it is the whole
// reason this arm exists. "The commit you asked me to walk from is not in this history" and "commit
// c's parent p is missing" have OPPOSITE OWNERS -- the first is a bad argument, the second is a
// truncated history -- and opposite remedies. Collapsing them additionally forced the walk to invent
// a `missing_parent`, since an unknown start supplies no parent to name: the fabricated-plausible-
// output failure at the boundary this module exists to be trustworthy over.
//
// IT IS REACHABLE ONLY FROM THE START, BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by convention. The step advances
// the cursor only after `lookup(parent)` has already answered AncestryKnown, so every cursor after
// the first is a commit this history holds. The unverified cursor is the starting one and no other.
//
// AncestryRevisitsACommit is a REFUSAL, not a truncation. A parent chain that returns to a commit it
// already visited is a cycle, and DESIGN's substrate is bounded and forward -- cyclic relations are
// encoded acyclically, never traversed as cyclic values. Detected by exhausting the population: a
// chain that takes more steps than the history has commits must have revisited one, by pigeonhole,
// so the refusal is DERIVED from the bound rather than asserted by a visited-set this module would
// have to keep correct.
type AncestryWalk
  = AncestryTraced { chain: List<RepositoryCommitRef> }
  | AncestryBrokenAt { commit: RepositoryCommitRef, missing_parent: RepositoryCommitRef }
  | AncestryStartNotInHistory { start: RepositoryCommitRef }
  | AncestryRevisitsACommit { detected_from: RepositoryCommitRef }

// IT CARRIES THE RESOLVED ROW, NOT A BARE REFERENCE, and that is what removes an arm rather than
// handling it. With a bare cursor the step had to look the cursor up again every iteration and answer
// for a not-found case that the walk's own invariant makes impossible -- an arm with no producer,
// which the previous shape filled with `AncestryStartNotInHistory` even mid-walk, where the cursor is
// not the start. Holding the row means the only lookup is the PARENT's, whose absence is a real and
// nameable state.
type AncestryWalkAcc {
  chain: List<RepositoryCommitRef>
  current: AncestryStep
  done: Bool
  outcome: AncestryWalk
}

// THE CHAIN ACCUMULATES BY PREPEND AND IS REVERSED ONCE AT COMPLETION. concat-at-end copies the
// whole accumulator per step, which makes an n-step walk O(n^2) -- the cost shape DESIGN section 6
// rules is ALWAYS fixed regardless of the realized n, because "n is small here" is not a time-stable
// fact: this walk's n is the length of a repository's history, which is exactly the quantity that
// grows without bound in use.
//
// It is worth recording that gunbc.scm.repository_envelope carries an annotation about having
// committed this same defect and had it caught by review, and this module reintroduced it anyway in
// a diff that imports that module. The lesson there was that fixing a cost shape at the sites a
// review names is not the same as not having the defect; the lesson here is that reading the note is
// not either. The reversal is at the single completion point rather than at each consumer, so the
// exported chain order -- descendant first -- is unchanged and no caller can observe the fix.
fn ancestry_walk_step(acc: AncestryWalkAcc, history: RepositoryHistory) -> AncestryWalkAcc {
  if acc.done {
    acc
  } else {
    match acc.current.ancestry {
      RootCommit =>
        AncestryWalkAcc {
          chain: acc.chain,
          current: acc.current,
          done: true,
          outcome: AncestryTraced { chain: reverse(acc.chain) },
        }
      DescendsFrom { parent: parent } =>
        match history_step_at(history: history, at: parent) {
          Absent =>
            AncestryWalkAcc {
              chain: acc.chain,
              current: acc.current,
              done: true,
              outcome: AncestryBrokenAt { commit: acc.current.at, missing_parent: parent },
            }
          Present { value: next } =>
            AncestryWalkAcc {
              chain: concat([parent], acc.chain),
              current: next,
              done: false,
              outcome: acc.outcome,
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

// The walk is bounded by the population it walks: `history` supplies both the membership test and
// the step budget, so the fold is total and terminates by construction rather than by a guard this
// module has to keep correct.
// THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO BOOLEAN ANCESTOR PREDICATE HERE, and its absence is the point.
//
// A `commit_is_ancestor_of` returning Bool answered `false` for FOUR different facts: a completed
// trace proving the candidate is not an ancestor, a broken parent chain, a start outside the
// history, and a cycle. Only the first is a negative ANSWER; the other three are failures to
// establish one. Collapsing them is the not-established-versus-negative conflation DESIGN names,
// and it is the same shape repaired across the JSON and source-analysis lanes.
//
// It is DELETED rather than typed, on consumer count. There is no production consumer -- the walk
// itself is the surface, and `AncestryWalk` already carries all four states distinctly. Publishing
// a typed `AncestorJudgment` today would mint an arm with no producer, which DESIGN section 4b
// rules worse than absent because it gets cited as a capability the model does not have. The same
// reasoning deleted `ancestry_is_root` from this module.
//
// WHEN A REAL CONSUMER ARRIVES -- merge-base, ahead/behind, a publication decision -- it derives its
// answer from `ancestry_walk` and decides for itself what a broken chain means to IT, which is a
// judgment this module cannot make on its behalf.

fn ancestry_walk(start: RepositoryCommitRef, history: RepositoryHistory) -> AncestryWalk {
  match history_step_at(history: history, at: start) {
    Absent => AncestryStartNotInHistory { start: start }
    Present { value: first } =>
      match fold(
        history.steps,
        init: AncestryWalkAcc {
          chain: [start],
          current: first,
          done: false,
          outcome: AncestryRevisitsACommit { detected_from: start },
        },
        f: fn(acc, _budget) { ancestry_walk_step(acc: acc, history: history) },
      ) {
        AncestryWalkAcc { chain: _, current: _, done: done, outcome: outcome } =>
          if done {
            outcome
          } else {
            AncestryRevisitsACommit { detected_from: start }
          }
      }
  }
}
